{"acronym":null,"arm_groups":[],"brief_summary":"The purpose of this study is to evaluate if type 2 diabetics who have inadequate glycemic control on metformin alone, have a similar, or not inferior, glycemic response when treated with the combination of muraglitazar and metformin compared to pioglitazone and metformin.","brief_title":"A Study to Evaluate Combining Metformin With Muraglitazar or Pioglitazone in Type 2 Diabetics","collaborators":[],"completion_date":null,"conditions":["Type 2 Diabetes"],"design":{"allocation":"RANDOMIZED","intervention_model":"PARALLEL","masking":"DOUBLE","primary_purpose":"TREATMENT","who_masked":[]},"eligibility":{"criteria":"Inclusion Criteria:\n\n* Type 2 diabetics\n* HbA1c ≥7.0% and ≤10.0%,currently receiving a stable dose of metformin 1500 to 2550 mg/day for at least 6 weeks prior to screening were enrolled in this study.\n* Fasting C-peptide ≥1.0 ng/mL\n* BMI≤41 kg/m2 mean fasting serum trig. ≤600 g/dL\n\nExclusion Criteria:\n\n* symptomatic type 2 diabetics with \\> 10% weight loss 3 months prior to study\n* history of diabetic ketoacidosis, hyperosmolar nonketotic coma, insulin therapy, inability to take muraglitazar, pioglitazone, or metformin according to investigator brochure or labeling\n* History of MI (myocardial infarction), coronary angioplasty or bypass graft(s), valvular disease or repair, unstable angina pectoris, transient ischemic attack (TIA), cerebrovascular attack, or cerebrovascular accident (CVA) within 6 months, congestive heart failure (NYHA Class III and IV, uncontrolled hypertension, history of, or renal disease, peripheral vascular disease (PVD), pulmonary disease, gastrointestinal disease, active liver disease or endocrine disease.","healthy_volunteers":false,"maximum_age":"70 Years","minimum_age":"18 Years","sex":"ALL","std_ages":["ADULT","OLDER_ADULT"]},"enrollment":{"count":1159,"type":null},"first_posted":"2005-10-18","has_results":false,"interventions":[{"name":"Muraglitazar","other_names":[],"ty